		}
		{
			dependencies: ["6AF9FD70EB5A15AB"]
			description: [
				"&eXNet&r takes the opposite approach to SFM: instead of a program, you get a &especification&r."
				""
				"The &eController&r is the brain. Every machine on the network gets a connector, every connector joins a coloured &echannel&r, and each channel says what moves, in which direction, through what filter, at what priority."
				""
				"One GUI, one screen, the whole base's logistics laid out as a table. Change a priority and it takes effect instantly."
			]
			icon: "xnet:controller"
			id: "5691D2336F4242E7"
			rewards: [{
				id: "05D2CC3C77448F32"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			shape: "gear"
			subtitle: "&7The whole base on one screen"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1604CD2866BF57E7"
				item: "xnet:controller"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bThe XNet Controller"
			x: -8.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5691D2336F4242E7"]
			description: [
				"&eNetwork Cable&r joins things to the Controller. &eConnectors&r are what actually touch a machine and decide what that machine contributes to a channel."
				""
				"&c&lWorth knowing:&r XNet has no plain uncoloured cable or connector you can craft - every one comes in &bblue&r, &agreen&r, &cred&r or &eyellow&r. The colour is purely organisational; pick one and stay consistent, or dye them later."
				""
				"A single cable run can carry every channel at once, so one backbone is usually all a base ever needs."
			]
			icon: "xnet:connector_blue"
			id: "721D4965AD3FB2EE"
			rewards: [{
				id: "6819589228EADB85"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
